@NODE OVERVIEW "About AmiMAIL"
AmiMAIL is a native general-purpose IMAP/SMTP mail client for AmigaOS 3.2+.
Unlike AmiGmail, AmiMAIL is not restricted to Gmail and can be configured
for normal IMAP/SMTP providers.

AmiMAIL intentionally uses a single-account design. The interface is based
on ReAction and encrypted network connections use AmiSSL.

The focus is classic Amiga operation, modest resource requirements and a
robust plain-text-oriented mail workflow.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE REQUIREMENTS "Requirements"
- AmigaOS 3.2 or newer
- 68020 or newer
- working TCP/IP stack
- AmiSSL 5
- IMAP/SMTP account at a supported mail provider

A generous Workbench stack is recommended. The supplied program icon uses a
stack size of 100000 bytes.

The Amiga date and time should be correct so TLS certificates can be
validated reliably.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE INSTALL "Installation"
Start the supplied Installer and select the existing drawer where AmiMAIL
should be installed.

After language selection, the Installer can optionally copy the whole
Setup/Notifications drawer to SYS:Prefs/Presets/Notifications.

AmiSSL 5 must already be installed correctly. The Installer does not replace
or update AmiSSL.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ACCOUNT "First start and account"
On first use, open Account settings and enter the information supplied by
your mail provider.

- Name: visible sender name; it is sent as Name <address> in the From:
  header.
- E-mail address: the address used as the message sender.
- IMAP server, port, username and password.
- SMTP server, port and, if needed, separate SMTP credentials.

AmiMAIL can use the same credentials for SMTP and IMAP, or retain separate
SMTP credentials.

Credentials are stored locally in protected form. A master password can be
requested at startup. Optionally, AmiMAIL can persist a derived key so the
master-password requester is skipped on later starts.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE SERVERS "IMAP/SMTP and TLS"
AmiMAIL supports direct TLS connections and, depending on the configured
service, STARTTLS. Required ports and TLS mode depend on your mail provider.

For Gmail, the tested configuration is IMAP 993 with direct TLS and SMTP 587
with STARTTLS. Other providers require their own settings.

When connecting, AmiMAIL tries all IPv4 addresses returned for the server.
TLS failures report the SSL error type, socket error and, where available,
negotiated TLS version and cipher.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE FOLDERS "Mapping system folders"
AmiMAIL is not tied to one provider's folder names. INBOX is the IMAP Inbox;
Sent, Trash and Drafts can be mapped to the folders used by your server.

Manual mapping is important for providers that use names such as Sent Items,
Trash, Deleted Messages or different Drafts folders.

The mapping affects draft storage, sent-message copies, deletion and the
related main-window actions.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE MAINWINDOW "The main window"
The folder list is on the left. The message list is in the upper-right area
and the message preview is below it.

The message list uses the native ListBrowser vertical ReAction prop. The
preview is directly coupled to its ReAction scroller through BOOPSI. Both
support mouse-wheel scrolling.

The main window stays fully interactive while the Compose window is open.
Message selection, preview updates, manual fetch and network events continue
to be processed.

Window position and size are saved on normal exit.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE READING "Reading and managing mail"
Select a message to read it in the lower preview area. AmiMAIL supports the
usual IMAP actions such as read/unread state, moving and deleting messages.

If the server supports UID MOVE, AmiMAIL uses it. Older servers use safe
COPY/DELETE variants without performing an unsafe mailbox-wide EXPUNGE.

Reply, Reply All and Forward are available from the Reply button and its
adjacent selection area.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE COMPOSE "Compose, reply and forward"
New Mail opens the Compose window with To, CC, BCC, Subject, message body
and attachments.

The configured name is written as the visible sender name in the
RFC-compliant From: header. The SMTP envelope still uses only the plain
e-mail address.

Reply All filters the user's own address and duplicate recipients. Forward
creates a new message without prefilled recipients and can retain existing
attachments.

The main window remains active while composing.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE DRAFTS "Drafts"
Drafts can be stored on the IMAP server and reopened later.

To, CC, BCC, Subject, body text, thread references and attachments are
restored when editing a draft.

Saving first appends the replacement draft and only then removes the old
one. Sending removes the old draft only after SMTP delivery succeeds.
Cancelling leaves the server draft unchanged.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ATTACHMENTS "Attachments"
AmiMAIL supports up to 8 attachments per message. The combined attachment
size is limited to 10 MB.

Attachments from received messages can be saved from the preview. Existing
attachments can be retained while forwarding or editing drafts.

Inline images from HTML mail are not automatically loaded.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE HTMLMAIL "HTML mail"
AmiMAIL does not contain a full HTML browser. A clean text/plain part is
preferred when available.

HTML-only messages are converted locally to plain text. Paragraphs, line
breaks, lists and links are retained where practical.

CSS, style/script/head content, tracking pixels and other external content
are not loaded. HTML entities and numeric entities are decoded.

For broken multipart/alternative messages whose supposed plain-text part is
actually generated CSS/HTML debris, AmiMAIL can automatically use the HTML
alternative and generate clean plain text from it.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE CONTACTS "Contacts and signature"
Edit -> Contact management... opens the local address book. Contacts can be
created, edited and deleted, and CSV or VCard files can be imported.

Contacts can be added to To, CC and BCC from a multi-selection list in the
Compose window.

Edit -> Signature... manages the local signature. It is inserted
automatically in new messages, replies, Reply All, forwards and mailto:
drafts.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE NOTIFY "Notifications and iconify"
AmiMAIL can play a configured notification sound through AmigaOS DataTypes
when new mail arrives.

The main window can be iconified. The network worker, periodic fetch and
status handling continue in the background.

The current Inbox state is exposed during runtime through the AmiMAILStatus
environment variable.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE INTEGRATION "mailto: and updates"
AmiMAIL supports mailto: URIs containing recipient, CC, BCC, Subject and
Body. If AmiMAIL is already running, the URI is handed to the existing
instance instead of starting a second mail session.

The GitHub update check runs asynchronously and at most once per program
start. An available release can be downloaded to RAM:; installation and
unpacking remain manual.

Browsers such as IBrowse can be configured to use AmiMAIL as an external
mailto: handler.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE KEYBOARD "Keyboard operation"
- Return/Enter confirms the primary OK/Apply action in requesters.
- Escape closes or cancels dialogs that provide a cancel action.
- TAB follows the ReAction focus order in input windows.
- The mouse wheel scrolls the message list and message preview.

The exact response of some keys also depends on the currently active
ReAction gadget.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE TROUBLE "Troubleshooting"
For connection errors, first compare server name, port, TLS mode and
credentials with the settings supplied by the mail provider.

- Verify TCP/IP connectivity and DNS resolution.
- Check AmiSSL 5 and the system date/time.
- Use a Google App Password when connecting to Gmail.
- For SSL_ERROR_SYSCALL or connection reset by peer, test the same server
  with openssl s_client.
- For Sent/Trash/Drafts problems, verify the system-folder mapping.

AmiMAIL provides detailed TLS diagnostics where possible. Include those
details together with TCP/IP stack, AmiSSL version and server configuration
in bug reports.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ABOUT "About / Copyright"
AmiMAIL 1.5 - native general IMAP/SMTP mail client for AmigaOS 3.2+.

© 2026 Andreas "Andiweli" Stürmer

AmiMAIL uses ReAction, IMAP, SMTP and AmiSSL and intentionally follows a
single-account design.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE
